      "message": "the test is straight forward: instantiate two of them, make sure that changes to one affect the other.\n\nThat said, this sortof gets down into \"does python work\".\n\n\u003cobj\u003e.__dict__  is where all instance-level changes occur, and are referenced, e.g. when you do setattr(\u003cinstance\u003e, \"attr_name\", \"attr_value\") it is stored in \u003cinstance\u003e.__dict__\n\nIn this case we are rebinding the instance\u0027s __dict__ to the classes\u0027s __dict__. This has to be done first, but since we\u0027re not diving into a super class __init__ we can simply assign it here directly as the sole element in init.\n\nthe only reason we use super() is because of the __setattr__ defined to prevent accidental setting on the object itself (it should be relatively immutable without working really hard to change something).\n\nCan we test this? Absolutely. Does it need a test? maybe. I\u0027ll consider adding one as a followup.",
